THE GAP. B1/B2 are byte-identical 403s by design, so response assertions cannot see a PARTIAL reorder. Moving authorizeFellowWrite to sit between the owned-workshop lookup and the P11 gate leaves B1 and B2 both 403 and still identical, while restoring the owned-workshop existence oracle (that lookup answers differently for a workshop id owned by another Fellow). A full revert IS caught by B1 now that B owns its workshop; the partial reorder is not. The 22e33a1a per-handler slicing already materialises promoteHandler, so the fix costs three lines inside the existing B3 block.

ORDERING PROVEN STATICALLY ON THE CURRENT BYTES, by byte offset rather than by running anything. Promote handler slice is [40641, 54301) — app.post("/v1/sessions/:id/promote") at 40641, app.post("/v1/sessions/:id/close") at 54301. Within that slice:
  replayResponseBeforeMutablePreconditions  43543
  authorizeFellowWrite                      45111
  FROM workshop_objects WHERE workshop_id = ? AND session_id = ? AND fellow_id = ?   45782
  normHash                                  46649
43543 < 45111 < 45782 < 46649, i.e. replay -> authorization -> owned-workshop lookup -> P11. Each anchor is unique inside the slice: exactly one replay call, exactly one authorizeFellowWrite (36170 is the workshop handler), and the SQL string occurs exactly once in the whole file. normHash occurs twice in the slice, so use indexOf (first = 46649). The assertions below therefore pass on 22e33a1a/18ea685b as written; they are not speculative.

PATCH — test-only, apply verbatim inside the existing B3 block, immediately after the two expect(...Handler).toContain("return writeRefusedProblem();") lines. Do not touch router.ts.

    // yn9p source-order guard. B1/B2 are byte-identical by construction, so no
    // response assertion can observe a partial reorder. Pin the three-tier order
    // structurally: replay read, then authorization, then every content-derived
    // lookup. Moving authorization below either lookup restores an existence
    // oracle while leaving both 403s unchanged.
    const replayAt = promoteHandler.indexOf("replayResponseBeforeMutablePreconditions");
    const authorizeAt = promoteHandler.indexOf("authorizeFellowWrite");
    const ownedWorkshopAt = promoteHandler.indexOf(
      "FROM workshop_objects WHERE workshop_id = ? AND session_id = ? AND fellow_id = ?",
    );
    const normHashAt = promoteHandler.indexOf("normHash");
    expect(replayAt).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0);
    expect(authorizeAt).toBeGreaterThan(replayAt);
    expect(ownedWorkshopAt).toBeGreaterThan(authorizeAt);
    expect(normHashAt).toBeGreaterThan(authorizeAt);

Four assertions, no new imports, no new fixture, reusing promoteHandler. The first is the zdz.3 tier-1 invariant (replay must stay ahead of authorization; hoisting authorization above it would break replay of an already-committed write after scope narrowing). The last two are yn9p's tier-2-before-tier-3 edge for BOTH oracles.

